<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 1650 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">I Saw Three Ships</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi>I Saw Three Ships (Come Sailing In)</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old English Christmas carol about three ships sailing in on Christmas morning, loved especially in Cornwall.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English carol singers, especially in Cornwall</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>England (the earliest printed version possibly from Derbyshire)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>earliest printed version 17th century; modern words published 1833</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; earliest printed version 17th century, the modern words published by William Sandys in 1833)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/i-saw-three-ships">I Saw Three Ships</a> (Come Sailing In)&quot; is an English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a>, number 700 in the Roud Folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Index. The earliest printed version is from the 17th century, possibly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>. The song was probably known traditionally as &quot;As I Sat On a Sunny Bank&quot;, and it was particularly popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>. The words sung today are those of a version of nine verses published in 1833 by the English lawyer and antiquarian William Sandys.</p><p>The carol has the ships sail into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, although the nearest body of water is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a>, about 20 miles (32 km) away. Several explanations have been offered. The three ships are thought to go back to those that carried the purported relics of the Biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> Cathedral in the 12th century. Another possible reference is to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a>, whose coat of arms showed three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> on blue. A third suggestion is that the ships are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> of the Magi, since camels are often called &quot;ships of the desert&quot;.</p><p>Countless traditional versions have been collected, sung to many different melodies, as is usual with folk songs and carols. In the 1910s the English folklorists Cecil Sharp and Janet Blunt noted the tunes and words of dozens of versions, mainly in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In the early 1930s the American collector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> Carpenter recorded several slightly different English versions, several of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>, and in 1956 Peter Kennedy recorded a man named John Thomas singing it in Camborne, Cornwall. A 19th-century version has the three ships come sailing by on New Year's Day, with three pretty girls aboard who entertain at a wedding held that day.</p><p>The song also crossed the Atlantic. Jean Ritchie, a musician from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a>, was recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-lomax">Alan Lomax</a> in 1949 singing a version learned from her Kentucky family, whose ancestors seemingly brought it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>; she recorded it again on her 1959 album Carols of All Seasons. Hers is the only traditional American version to have been recorded, though the song was known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in earlier decades, particularly in the south. In 1952, collecting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> with her husband George Pickow, Ritchie met the Irish traditional singer Elizabeth Cronin in Macroom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-cork">County Cork</a>, who sang a version called &quot;The Bells of Heaven&quot;.</p><p>The carol is a staple of choirs. An arrangement by Martin Shaw appears in The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> Book of Carols, and the Carols for Choirs books have arrangements by Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Willcocks and by John Rutter. Simon Preston and Sir Philip Ledger wrote arrangements that the Choir of King's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, has sung at the Festival of Nine Lessons and Carols. It has also been recorded by popular musicians, among them Nat King Cole in 1960, Jon Anderson in 1985 and Sting in 1997, and is used as a counterpoint in the musical Caroline, or Change.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The singer sees three ships sail in on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> morning, carrying Christ and his mother to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, and calls on bells, angels and all people to rejoice. It is a bright, dancing carol built on repeated lines.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I saw three ships come sailing in</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day, on Christmas day;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I saw three ships come sailing in</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day in the morning.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And what was in those ships all three,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day, on Christmas day?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And what was in those ships all three,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day in the morning?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our Saviour Christ and his lady,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day, on Christmas day;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our Saviour Christ and his lady,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day in the morning.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Pray whither sailed those ships all three,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day, on Christmas day?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Pray whither sailed those ships all three,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day in the morning?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O they sailed into Bethlehem,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day, on Christmas day;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O they sailed into Bethlehem,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day in the morning.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all the bells on earth shall ring,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day, on Christmas day;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all the bells on earth shall ring,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day in the morning.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all the Angels in Heaven shall sing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day, on Christmas day;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all the Angels in Heaven shall sing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day in the morning.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all the Souls on Earth shall sing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day, on Christmas day;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all the Souls on Earth shall sing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day in the morning.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then let us all rejoice amain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day, on Christmas day;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then let us all rejoice amain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On Christmas day in the morning.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; as printed in William Sandys, Christmas Carols, Ancient and Modern (1833). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Christmas_Carols%2C_Ancient_and_Modern%2FI_Saw_Three_Ships" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
